Detailed Findings

🔐 1. SQL Injection

Severity: Critical

Description:
User-supplied input was improperly handled within backend SQL queries, allowing direct manipulation of database logic.

Impact:

Unauthorized access to database data

PotentialRisk of data modification or deletion

Potential full database compromise

Business Risk:

Data breach

Regulatory and compliance exposure

Loss of customer trust and reputational damage

Recommendation:

Implement parameterized queries

Enforce strict server-side input validation

Suppress database error messages in production

🔐 2. Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)

Severity: Medium

Description:
User input was reflected in application responses without proper sanitization or encoding, allowing execution of client-side scripts.

Impact:

Session hijacking

User impersonation

Client-side manipulation

Business Risk:

User account compromise

Increased phishing and fraud risk

Brand reputation damage

Recommendation:

Apply output encoding based on context

Implement Content Security Policy (CSP)

Validate and sanitize all user inputs

🔐 3. Session Management Weaknesses

Severity: Low to Medium

Description:
Session cookies lacked consistent security attributes, and session invalidation behavior was inconsistent.

Impact:

Increased susceptibility to session hijacking

Elevated risk when combined with XSS

Business Risk:

Unauthorized account access

Reduced platform trust

Recommendation:

Enforce Secure and HTTPOnly cookie flags

Regenerate session identifiers after login

Implement session timeouts

Risk Prioritization

Based on likelihood and impact, remediation should be prioritized as follows:

Immediate: SQL Injection

Short Term: Cross-Site Scripting

Planned Improvement: Session hardening

Addressing the critical vulnerability significantly reduces overall application risk.

Overall Risk Assessment -

The audit demonstrates that a small number of critical flaws can expose an application to severe business impact.
Organizations should prioritize fixing high-impact vulnerabilities rather than focusing solely on vulnerability counts.

Regular security assessments and secure development practices are essential to maintaining a strong security posture.

